 such as, “If you don’t want it, I’ll give it to your little brother,” which may be effective with children aged two or three. Ultimately, it will be necessary to determine what strategies work best for your particular child. Engaging in conversations with other parents can also provide helpful inspiration.\n\nSeek out activities that both you and your children can enjoy together. Perhaps you both appreciate sports or a specific genre of film. Alternatively, your child may have an interest that aligns with something you care about, offering you the opportunity to derive enjoyment from teaching them. If you are fond of video games, you may consider allowing them to use the controller (if they are young, they may not even notice that it is turned off). It is important to remember that children are often impulsive. They may express a desire for something, such as a pet, but quickly change their minds once the novelty wears off or when they realize the responsibility involved. Therefore, it is advisable to refrain from making major commitments immediately until their level of interest becomes evident. This can help prevent unnecessary frustration.\n\nFinally, general strategies for relaxation and improved well-being remain effective even in the context of parenthood. For example, making it a nightly practice to list three positive events that occurred during the day may contribute to a greater sense of happiness and reduced stress, as this practice encourages a focus on the positive aspects of life. If time permits, consider participating in a yoga class to unwind—there are even classes specifically designed for parents with infants. Occasionally, all that is needed is a shift in perspective.", "original_formality": "formal", "bertscore_cf": 0.8359787464141846, "bertscore_dr": 0.7725207805633545, "nli_cf": 0.06494470685720444, "nli_dr": 0.6356967091560364, "passed_cf": false, "passed_dr": false, "score_original": 6, "score_counterfactual": 7, "score_double_rewrite": 7}
